  "query_preview": "..(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 510 AM EDT SUN AUG 2 2026\n\nThe first part of the extended will be dealing with the rare 5h\nlow over the Deep South as it gradually fills but not before it \nkeeps things unsettled for eastern Kentucky through at least mid \nweek. A more active jet stream to the north follows into the \nupcoming weekend but whether any systems can take advantage of \nthis to reach into Kentucky and help to clear out the leftover \nmoisture remains to be seen. As such, we anticipated unsettled \nactivity through the rest of the week though adhering to more of a\ndiurnal pattern.\n\nThe previous long term discussion follows:\n\nThe long wave pattern will remain stagnant and amplified over the \nCONUS next week. Two strong upper level high pressure systems will \nbe centering over Bermuda and the Four Corners region with time. \nMeanwhile, a slow-moving trough will initially be laid out near or \njust west of the Appalachians, keeping unsettled weather in place \nacross the region early next week. The trough will dampen by mid-\nweek, as higher geopotential heights spread west towards the \nEastern Seaboard from the Bermuda high center. In tandem, higher \ngeopotential heights will work east of the Mississippi River from\nthe Four Corners high center. Additionally, cyclonic flow will \noverspread the north central CONUS from a deeper upper level low \nthat will be gyring over Hudson Bay. This will likely result in \nany frontal systems remaining generally north of the Ohio Valley \ninto next weekend, and while diurnally-influenced PoPs will \nremain in the forecast, coverage will likely be more scattered in \nnature for the latter half of next week. \n\nEastern Kentucky will start out under the influence of an upper \nlevel trough, keeping higher rain chances in place through at \nleast Tuesday. PWATs will not be excessively high (LREF mean \nvalues of 50-75th percentile of climatology); however, high \nfreezing levels and slower storm motions of 10 to 15 kts will keep\nat least a marginal heavy rainfall threat in place at times. \nTemperatures will average near seasonal normals for early August, \nwith highs in the lower 80s and lows in the mid 60s, with \nelevated humidity levels. As we come more under the influence of \nthe ridging aloft, rain chances will generally decrease, with \nconvection becoming more scattered in nature each day, especially \nfrom Thursday through Saturday. Temperatures will creep up a bit\nthrough next weekend, peaking in the mid to upper 80s. Lows will \nalso moderate through the upper 60s to near 70, with higher \nhumidity levels continuing across the area.",
